; THE FIXED-LIMB RUNG, WRITTEN IN OROBOROS.
;
; ADR 0019's ladder has four rungs and this is the third: narrower than a word,
; the host's word, FIXED LIMBS, and the host's own bignum. A range that is
; FINITE and above the portable window gives a limb count, which gives a `build`
; of known length, which gives ZERO ALLOCATIONS -- bigarith-2026-08-28 measured
; that at 3.97x over `math/big`, 6.2x over `BigInteger` and 5.8x over `BigInt`,
; and on the last two it is the ONLY way there, because those bignums are
; immutable.
;
; It is a LIBRARY and not compiler code, for the reason `emit/winmap.oro` is:
; adding a case to `emit/*.go` for something that is a library is the single
; most common way to get this architecture wrong. It is embedded rather than
; sitting in `lib/` because a program must not have to import it -- a range is
; a declaration, not a dependency.
;
; ═══ BASE 2^24, AND THE BASE IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN
;
; A limb product plus the accumulated carry has to stay EXACT, and ADR 0012 says
; an `int` is exact to +-(2^53-1). With W-bit limbs a product is under 2^2W, so
; 2W < 53 and W <= 26. Taking W = 24 leaves four bits of headroom:
;
;   out[i+j] < 2^24, a[i]*b[j] < 2^48, carry < 2^25
;   t = out + a*b + carry  <  2^48 + 2^25 + 2^24  <  2^49
;
; ═══ `/` AND `%`, NOT A SHIFT AND A MASK, AND THAT IS THE LANGUAGE SPEAKING
;
; The natural carry split is `t >> 24` and `t & 0xFFFFFF`. Bitwise operators are
; DELIBERATELY not in the language (integers.md §0a): V8 coerces both operands
; to int32, so `(2^32) & -1` is 0 there and 4294967296 on the other three -- an
; observable disagreement INSIDE the portable window, which is what makes a
; construct Tier 2.
;
; So this uses `/` and `%`, which all four hosts agree on. bigarith measured the
; cost: bitwise carry extraction is worth 3.9x at equal storage on V8. That is
; the price of writing the library ONCE instead of four times, and naming it
; here is what makes it a decision rather than an oversight.
;
; ═══ THE WIDTH IS A DECLARATION, SO EXCEEDING IT MUST BE LOUD
;
; This is the rung's whole difficulty. The host's bignum is exact whatever the
; declared range says, so an under-declared bound costs nothing there. A fixed
; width TRUNCATES -- and then selecting a representation would change the
; answer, which is ADR 0009's rule at a different boundary and the one thing
; this project refuses.
;
; So every operation checks its final carry with `trap-if`, which every target
; can express: `panic` on Go, `throw` on JavaScript and the JVM, `ud2` on x86.
; One comparison per OPERATION, not per limb.
;
; That makes the two upper rungs a real choice rather than a detail:
;
;   (int 0 (pow 2 1300))   fixed width, no allocation, traps at the bound
;   (int 0 +inf)           the host's bignum, exact, allocates
;
; ═══ WHAT IS AND IS NOT HERE
;
; Non-negative magnitudes only. `(int 0 N)` and `(int 0 +inf)` are the
; declarations this serves and both are non-negative -- which is what
; unbounded-rung.md §4.2 means by a half-open range carrying something the
; representation can spend, since bigarith measured sign handling as a real
; cost.
;
; HERE: addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division by a machine WORD.
; Subtraction returns a magnitude and TRAPS on underflow, for the same reason
; addition traps on overflow -- a negative result is outside the `(int 0 N)` a
; program declared to reach this rung.
;
; NOT HERE, and a program using one falls back to the host bignum whole (or is
; refused by name on a target with none): the REMAINDER, whose result is a word
; rather than a limb table and so a change of type; the COMPARISONS; and
; big-by-big DIVISION, which needs a quotient estimate.

(module big/limb)

; A CLAMP, exactly as tree.oro, winmap.oro and kara/core.oro use one. Every
; index here is derived from a loop bound over a DIFFERENT table than the one
; being read -- two operands under one bound, which is the latent bug the
; refinement layer found in `dot` and `centroid`. The tables have the same
; length by construction and nothing can see that, so the program says what
; happens if they do not.
; AND THE VALUE IS MASKED, not only the index, which is the honest limit rather
; than a shortcut.
;
; A limb is under 2^24 by construction, but that is an INDUCTIVE INVARIANT over a
; buffer's own contents and frozen-2026-08-28 says such a thing is strictly
; stronger than an octagon. Inside a `build` lambda every operand table is FREE,
; so a read of one is stratum 0 and the analysis correctly refuses to claim a
; range — and without a mask every column sum here is unbounded and the whole
; library is refused by bounded-by-default.
;
; `% 2^24` is the identity on every value this library stores, so it changes no
; answer; it costs one division per limb read. Removing it needs the analysis to
; carry a frozen buffer's element range through a substitution into a lambda
; where the buffer is free, which is a real extension and is named rather than
; assumed.
;
; It has to be here rather than at the call sites because a LIBRARY is applied to
; whatever the program has: `examples/big/limbs.oro` reads a loop variable, whose
; element range the analysis does join, and a big literal's spine reads a `build`
; whose range does not survive being substituted in. One of those proves and the
; other does not, and a library may not be provable only sometimes.
; AND THE RESULT IS NON-NEGATIVE BY CONSTRUCTION, which costs one compare and
; buys 2.39x.
;
; `%` takes the DIVIDEND's sign, so masking a value the analysis cannot bound
; gives back `[-(2^24-1), 2^24-1]` -- symmetric around zero. Every column sum
; built from it is then symmetric too, and `t / 2^24` on a possibly-negative
; value is not a shift: truncation toward zero needs a rounding correction, and
; Go, the JVM and x86 all emit one.
;
; So the read says it is non-negative, and the whole carry chain follows: the
; column sum, the stored limb and the outgoing carry are all provably >= 0, and
; `SelectShifts` turns every `/` and `%` in this file into a shift and a mask.
; Measured at 2.39x on the factorial (shiftdiv-2026-09-03), which is larger than
; the clamp, this mask and the buffer clear PUT TOGETHER -- those three are
; inside the noise floor.
;
; It is a fact the program states rather than one the analysis derives, and the
; honest version is still owed: a limb IS non-negative by the buffer's element
; range, which is an inductive invariant over a buffer's own contents, and
; frozen-2026-08-28 says that is strictly stronger than an octagon.
(def at (fn (t i)
  (let (if (< i 0) 0 (if (>= i (len t)) 0 (t i))) (fn (v)
    (if (< v 0) 0 (% v 16777216))))))

; THE BOUND CHECK, and it is why this rung is sound at all. `trap-if` returns 0
; when handed 0 and fails loudly otherwise; both branches of the conditional are
; the buffer, so the check costs a compare and changes nothing when it passes.
;
; It is written as a conditional rather than a `seq` because `seq` needs an
; impure operation to sequence, and a trap that only ever fires is pure in the
; only sense that matters here: it returns the same thing every time it returns.
;
; TWO HALVES, AND THE SECOND IS WHAT MAKES THE TWO REPRESENTATIONS AGREE. The
; carry `c` catches a value past the buffer's LAST limb; `lim` catches one past
; the DECLARED bound, which can sit up to 23 bits lower. The host's bignum
; enforces the bound at its bit length (`big-fit`), and a declaration that meant
; one thing under limbs and another under `BigInt` would be ADR 0009's rule
; broken at the representation boundary -- the compiler would be choosing the
; answer, not just the storage.
;
; When the bound IS a whole number of limbs, `lim` is 2^24 and a limb is under
; 2^24 by construction, so the check is present, free, and never fires. That is
; the right shape for a check whose job is to make two representations agree.
(def guard (fn (n lim c o)
  (if (= (trap-if (+ c (if (< (at o (- n 1)) lim) 0 1))) 0) o o)))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- of a word
;
; A machine word spread over n limbs, least significant first. `lim` is taken
; and IGNORED, so that every entry point has one shape and the splice does not
; have to know which of them checks.
;
; No check is needed here, and it is a proof rather than an oversight: a range
; reaches this rung only by being ABOVE the portable window, so the declared
; bound is at least 54 bits, and a machine word is under 2^53. A word can
; therefore never exceed the bound it is being widened into.
(def of (fn (n lim v)
  (build n (fn (b)
    (loop ((b b) (i 0) (r v))
      (>= i n)  b
      else      (again (set b i (% r 16777216)) (+ i 1) (/ r 16777216)))))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- addition
;
; The carry rides as an ordinary loop variable, which is what makes this
; expressible: ADR 0015 lets `again` sit under a `let`, so the column sum is
; named once and used for both the stored limb and the outgoing carry.
(def add (fn (n lim a b)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i 0) (c 0))
      (>= i n)  (guard n lim c o)
      else      (let (+ (+ (at a i) (at b i)) c) (fn (t)
                  (again (set o i (% t 16777216)) (+ i 1) (/ t 16777216)))))))))

; ------------------------------------------------------- multiply by a word
;
; a * k for a machine-word k, which is what a factorial is made of and the case
; bigarith measured our form winning by 3.97x. One pass, one carry.
;
; k is NOT assumed to fit a limb: `(at a i) * k` with k up to 2^24 stays under
; 2^48, and the lowering only chooses this form when the multiplier is a widened
; word whose range the analysis has bounded.
(def mul-small (fn (n lim a k)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i 0) (c 0))
      (>= i n)  (guard n lim c o)
      else      (let (+ (* (at a i) k) c) (fn (t)
                  (again (set o i (% t 16777216)) (+ i 1) (/ t 16777216)))))))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------ big by big
;
; Schoolbook, one row at a time, with the row's carry rippling as a loop
; variable. `row` accumulates a[i]*b[j] into out[i+j]; the outer loop walks i.
;
; The read-back of `o` goes through `at` like every other read, so it carries the
; same mask -- and here the buffer being read is the one being BUILT, which is
; stratum 0 for a second reason.
; THE COLUMN PAST THE LAST LIMB IS AN OVERFLOW, NOT A WRAP, and the first
; version of this got it wrong in the way a fixed width invites: it CLAMPED the
; store index, so a partial product at `i+j >= n` was written into slot 0 and
; the answer came back wrong in its low digits. A clamp says "do something
; safe"; here the only safe thing is to fail, because the declared width has
; been exceeded.
;
; So the row has three clauses. Past the end of the row, stop. Past the end of
; the RESULT, check that the contribution and the carry are both zero and keep
; going -- `guard` nests, so both are checked. Otherwise accumulate.
;
; The middle clause is also what discharges the store's bounds obligation in the
; last one: its negation gives `i + j < n`, and `n` is the buffer's length.
(def macrow (fn (n lim o a b i)
  (loop ((o o) (j 0) (c 0))
    (>= j n)          (guard n lim c o)
    (>= (+ i j) n)    (again (guard n lim (* (at a i) (at b j)) (guard n lim c o)) (+ j 1) 0)
    else              (let (+ (+ (at o (+ i j)) (* (at a i) (at b j))) c) (fn (t)
                        (again (set o (+ i j) (% t 16777216))
                               (+ j 1) (/ t 16777216)))))))

(def mul (fn (n lim a b)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i 0))
      (>= i n)  o
      else      (again (macrow n lim o a b i) (+ i 1)))))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- subtraction
;
; A BORROW CHAIN, which is addition's dual and needs nothing addition did not.
; ADR 0015 forbids `again` under an `if`, so the two outcomes are `let`-computed
; arguments to ONE `again` rather than two clauses -- the shape json/tree.oro
; found when five token classes became one clause.
;
; THE FINAL BORROW IS AN UNDERFLOW, and `guard` is already the right check for
; it: these are non-negative magnitudes, `(int 0 N)` is what a program declares
; to reach this rung, and `a - b` with a < b is a value outside that declaration
; exactly as an overflow is. So it traps, and for the same reason -- the
; alternative is a wrap, and then choosing a representation would change the
; answer (ADR 0009 at the representation boundary).
(def sub (fn (n lim a b)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i 0) (bw 0))
      (>= i n)  (guard n lim bw o)
      else      (let (- (- (at a i) (at b i)) bw) (fn (t)
                  (again (set o i (if (< t 0) (+ t 16777216) t))
                         (+ i 1)
                         (if (< t 0) 1 0)))))))))

; --------------------------------------------------------- divide by a word
;
; a / k for a machine-word k -- the exact dual of `mul-small`, one pass, one
; running remainder, and the operation DECIMAL RENDERING is made of: printing a
; limb table is repeated division by a power of ten.
;
; IT WALKS DOWNWARD, most significant limb first, because that is the direction
; a remainder propagates. Termination is by ORIENTATION (sct-2026-08-19): a
; descending counter is an ascending one under mu = -v, so the size-change
; witness is the same one an ordinary loop has.
;
; `cur` is `r * 2^24 + a[i]` with r < k, so it is under `k * 2^24 + 2^24` and the
; window admits it only for k under about 2^28. Nothing here says so and nothing
; needs to: bounded-by-default asks at the call site with the caller's own range
; for k in hand, and refuses if it cannot prove it. A library that stated its own
; limit would be stating a weaker one than the caller can.
;
; DIVISION BY ZERO IS THE CALLER'S OBLIGATION and is already discharged there:
; `(/ x k)` carries it before the promotion runs (integers.md §13.4), and
; `splice` normalises this body against the caller's actual argument, so the
; same facts are in scope at the same goal.
(def div-small (fn (n lim a k)
  (build n (fn (o)
    (loop ((o o) (i (- n 1)) (r 0))
      (< i 0)  o
      else     (let (+ (* r 16777216) (at a i)) (fn (cur)
                 (again (set o i (/ cur k)) (- i 1) (% cur k)))))))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- comparison
;
; Limb-wise from the TOP down: the first difference decides, and equality is
; reaching the bottom without finding one. Both operands have the same width by
; construction — one program holds one — so there is no length to compare first.
;
; WITHOUT THIS A BIGNUM CANNOT CONTROL A LOOP, which is an expressiveness gap
; rather than a slow path: no `while (x > 1)`, no gcd, no Newton iteration, no
; exponentiation that tests its own exponent. On a target that stores big values
; as limbs and has no host bignum to fall back to, all of that was unreachable.
;
; It returns -1, 0 or 1 rather than a bool so that one loop serves all five
; comparisons. Three guards read each limb twice, which is two compares more per
; limb than a difference would cost; a comparison walks limbs and stops at the
; first that differs, so this is not the arithmetic anyone measures.
(def cmp (fn (n lim a b)
  (loop ((i (- n 1)))
    (< i 0)                 0
    (> (at a i) (at b i))   1
    (< (at a i) (at b i))   (- 0 1)
    else                    (again (- i 1)))))

(def lt (fn (n lim a b) (<  (cmp n lim a b) 0)))
(def le (fn (n lim a b) (<= (cmp n lim a b) 0)))
(def gt (fn (n lim a b) (>  (cmp n lim a b) 0)))
(def ge (fn (n lim a b) (>= (cmp n lim a b) 0)))
(def eq (fn (n lim a b) (=  (cmp n lim a b) 0)))

; -------------------------------------------------------- remainder by a word
;
; `div-small`'s other half, and the two together are divmod — which is what
; DECIMAL RENDERING consumes: printing a limb table is repeatedly taking the
; quotient and the remainder by a power of ten.
;
; ITS RESULT IS A MACHINE WORD, not a limb table, which is the one place this
; library changes the KIND of thing an operation produces. `a % k` is under k
; and k is a word, so the answer is a word by construction — there is no limb
; count to give it and no `build` here.
(def rem-small (fn (n lim a k)
  (loop ((i (- n 1)) (r 0))
    (< i 0)  r
    else     (let (+ (* r 16777216) (at a i)) (fn (cur)
               (again (- i 1) (% cur k)))))))
